# LungMask-XAI-CXR

Chest X-ray classification (COVID-19 / Non-COVID pneumonia / Normal) with **lung-mask-verified explainability**.

> **Research / course project. Not a medical device and not for real diagnosis.**

---

## 1. What the system does

1. **Identify** – a classifier looks at a chest X-ray and says *COVID-19*, *Non-COVID pneumonia* or *Normal*, with a confidence value.
2. **Outline the lungs** – a lung segmentation model draws the lung area. *(planned)*
3. **Detect infection** – an infection segmentation model marks the infected regions and reports how much of the lungs is affected. *(planned)*
4. **Explain** – heatmaps (Grad-CAM, Score-CAM and others) show where the classifier looked. *(planned)*
5. **Check** – the system measures how much of each heatmap falls **inside the lungs** (and, for COVID-19, inside the infection area). If most attention is outside the lungs, the prediction is flagged as unreliable. *(planned, this is our main contribution)*
6. **App** – a small web app where a user uploads an X-ray and sees all of the above. *(planned)*

### Why this project
Two papers that use this dataset (LungMaxViT, and a knowledge-distillation paper) judge heatmaps only by eye, use one explanation method each, and do not use the dataset's lung masks to verify the explanation. We want to measure it, compare several explanation methods, and test whether a model relies on image-source quirks (borders, markers) instead of lungs.

---

## 2. Dataset: COVID-QU-Ex

- Kaggle: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/anasmohammedtahir/covidqu (see the Kaggle page for license and terms)
- 33,920 chest X-rays: COVID-19 (11,956), Non-COVID pneumonia (11,263), Normal (10,701). Images are 256x256 grayscale PNG.
- It contains two parts:
  - **Lung Segmentation Data**: all 33,920 images + lung masks.
  - **Infection Segmentation Data**: a subset of 5,826 images + lung masks + infection masks. All of these images also exist in the lung part.

### How it is split
The dataset ships with a fixed split, **which we keep unchanged** (about 64% train / 16% val / 20% test, the same split the papers use, so results stay comparable).

| Part | Train | Val | Test | Total |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| lung_seg | 21,715 | 5,417 | 6,788 | 33,920 |
| infection_seg | 3,728 | 932 | 1,166 | 5,826 |

Per class (lung_seg): train 7,658 / 7,208 / 6,849, val 1,903 / 1,802 / 1,712, test 2,395 / 2,253 / 2,140 (COVID-19 / Non-COVID / Normal). Classes are nearly balanced, so no class weighting is used.

### Folder layout after our restructuring
```
data/
  lung_seg/        {train,val,test}/{COVID-19,Non-COVID,Normal}/{images,lung_masks}
  infection_seg/   {train,val,test}/{COVID-19,Non-COVID,Normal}/{images,lung_masks,infection_masks}
  manifest.csv         one row per image (paths, class, split)
  manifest_clean.csv   same + 'issues' and 'keep' columns (used for training)
```
The images themselves are **not stored in this repo** (too large). Only `manifest_clean.csv` is.

---

## 3. Project structure
```
app/            web app (planned)
models/         saved model weights (not in git, see section 9)
outputs/        figures and result files
src/
  check_data.py               count files per folder
  restructure_data.py         flatten the nested Kaggle folders
  check_data_deep.py          pairing, sizes, mask values, duplicate check
  make_manifest.py            build data/manifest.csv + sample figure
  check_preprocessing_need.py measure pixel range, noise, brightness per class
  clean_data.py               flag bad files -> manifest_clean.csv
  dataset.py                  PyTorch Dataset + preprocessing + augmentation
  train_classifier.py         training script (runs on Kaggle GPU)
requirements.txt
```

---

## 4. How to open and run this project

### Get the code
```bash
git clone https://github.com/bushradev01/LungMask-XAI-CXR.git
cd LungMask-XAI-CXR
```

### Create the environment (Windows PowerShell)
```powershell
python -m venv venv
.\venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
# if scripts are blocked:  Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope Process -ExecutionPolicy Bypass
pip install -r requirements.txt
```
(macOS/Linux: `source venv/bin/activate`)

### Get the data (only needed for local checks)
1. Download the dataset from the Kaggle link above and extract it into `data/`.
2. Flatten the folders (dry run first, then apply). Use the name of the folder you extracted:
```powershell
python src/restructure_data.py --archive "data/<extracted folder name>"
python src/restructure_data.py --archive "data/<extracted folder name>" --apply
```
3. Re-create the lists and checks:
```powershell
python src/check_data_deep.py
python src/make_manifest.py
python src/clean_data.py
```
`manifest_clean.csv` is already in the repo, so step 3 is only needed if you want to reproduce it.

**No GPU?** Training runs on Kaggle (free GPU), so local data is only needed for checks. See section 6.

---

## 5. What we did in data preparation and preprocessing

**Checks (all passed):** image/mask files pair up in every folder; all images are 256x256 grayscale; lung masks are binary (0/255) and never empty; no identical images across splits; 0 corrupt files. In `clean_data.py` all 39,746 rows were checked, 0 were excluded, 4 lung masks were flagged as "very large" (kept).

**Findings that decided the preprocessing** (sample of 300 images per class):
- Pixels use the full 0-255 range, so scaling/normalization is required.
- Estimated noise is about 1 gray level out of 255, so **no denoising** is applied.
- Classes differ slightly in brightness (mean about 138 / 126 / 130 for COVID-19 / Non-COVID / Normal) and in border brightness (about 108 / 93 / 99). This hints that images come from different sources, so a model could pick up source quirks instead of lungs. This is one reason for the lung-mask check later.
- Infection masks are non-empty **only for COVID-19** images; Non-COVID and Normal infection masks are blank.

**Preprocessing pipeline (`src/dataset.py`, applied on the fly, nothing is saved to disk):**
1. Grayscale to 3 channels (pretrained models expect RGB).
2. Resize to 224x224.
3. Scale to 0-1 and normalize with ImageNet mean/std.
4. Training images only: rotation up to 10 degrees, zoom 0.9-1.1, brightness/contrast jitter. Horizontal flip is off by default (it would move the heart to the other side).
5. CLAHE contrast enhancement is available but **off by default** (planned as an ablation).

---

## 6. How to train (Kaggle)

1. Create a Kaggle dataset `dl-code` containing `src/dataset.py`, `src/train_classifier.py` and `data/manifest_clean.csv`.
2. Open https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/anasmohammedtahir/covidqu and click **New Notebook**, then **Add Input** -> `dl-code`.
3. Settings: Accelerator **GPU T4 x2**, Internet **On** (needed to download pretrained weights; Kaggle asks for phone verification).
4. Cells:
```
!pip install -q timm
```
```python
import os
for dp, dn, fn in os.walk("/kaggle/input"):
    if "train_classifier.py" in fn:
        print(os.path.join(dp, "train_classifier.py"))
    if dp.count(os.sep) >= 5:
        dn[:] = []
```
```
%run "PATH_PRINTED_ABOVE"
```
5. Choose the model in the CONFIG block at the top of `train_classifier.py`:
   `MODEL`, `BATCH`, `LR`, `EPOCHS`, `TRAIN_FRACTION` (use part of the training data), `FREEZE_BACKBONE`, `USE_CLAHE`.
6. **Save Version -> Save & Run All (Commit)** to run in the background. Results are in the Logs and Output tabs.

The script uses the official val split to pick the best epoch and evaluates on the test split **once** at the end (accuracy, macro F1, AUC, per-class report, confusion matrix).

---

## 7. Results so far

| Model | Year | Train images | Test accuracy | Macro F1 | Macro AUC |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| ConvNeXt-Tiny (ImageNet pretrained, full fine-tuning) | 2022 | 21,715 | **96.61%** | 0.9656 | 0.9956 |

Per class F1: COVID-19 0.986, Non-COVID 0.959, Normal 0.952.
Confusion matrix on the test set (rows = true, columns = predicted; order COVID-19, Non-COVID, Normal):
```
[[2341   20   34]
 [   4 2164   85]
 [   9   78 2053]]
```
Most errors are between Non-COVID and Normal, which is also the hardest case reported in the literature. Training took about 30 minutes on a Kaggle T4. Published papers report about 97% on this dataset, so this is the expected level.

---

## 8. What is next (roadmap)

- [x] Dataset study, restructuring and cleaning
- [x] Preprocessing and augmentation (`dataset.py`)
- [x] Classifier 1: ConvNeXt-Tiny (96.61%)
- [ ] Classifier 2: MaxViT-Tiny (`MODEL="maxvit_tiny_tf_224"`, `BATCH=32`)
- [ ] Classifier 3: a light 2023-2024 model, e.g. MobileNetV4 (`mobilenetv4_conv_small`, `BATCH=128`, `LR=5e-4`)
- [ ] Limited-data runs (`TRAIN_FRACTION` = 0.01 / 0.05 / 0.10) and a frozen-backbone run: accuracy vs training-set size
- [ ] Ablation: CLAHE on/off, and lung-masked images (background removed)
- [ ] Lung segmentation model (U-Net style, ConvNeXt encoder), trained on `lung_seg`
- [ ] Infection segmentation model, trained on `infection_seg` (COVID-19 only has non-empty infection masks)
- [ ] Heatmaps: Grad-CAM, Score-CAM and at least one more, on the same classifier
- [ ] Lung-overlap metric: fraction of heatmap energy inside the lung mask; for COVID-19, also overlap with the infection mask
- [ ] Web app in `app/` (upload X-ray -> prediction, lung outline, infection regions, heatmap, lung-check warning)
- [ ] Final report

---

## 9. Large files are not in git
Data, virtual environment and model weights (`*.pt`, over 100 MB) are ignored via `.gitignore`. Trained weights are shared through a link (Kaggle output / GitHub Release / Google Drive), see the Releases page or ask the repo owner.

---

## 10. Limitations
- The dataset has **no patient IDs**, so images of the same patient may appear in both train and test. This cannot be checked and may make accuracy look slightly higher.
- Infection masks exist only for COVID-19, so infection-overlap evaluation covers COVID-19 only.
- Non-COVID is a mixed class (viral and bacterial pneumonia).
- The dataset pools several sources; brightness differences between classes suggest possible source shortcuts.

---

## 11. References
- Dataset: COVID-QU-Ex (Qatar University and University of Dhaka), https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/anasmohammedtahir/covidqu
- Paper 1: LungMaxViT (Fu et al., Scientific Reports 2025), https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-90607-x
- Paper 2: explainable knowledge distillation (Mir and Rizvi), link to be added
- Related: arXiv 2508.15251, "ExplainCXR: Lung-Mask-Verified Explainable Deep Learning for Normal, Pneumonia and COVID-19 Detection from Chest X-rays", https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.15251 (check how it relates to this project)

